{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfwRr4gjnkjP3NW2PVKZM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard in Kibana or checked that the rule has run. The rule's status was still \"pending\" when it was created.\n\n**What the data holds:** `kibana_sample_data_logs` is web access logs, with `timestamp` as the time field. The fields I used are:\n- `response` (the status code, stored as a string)\n- `extension`\n- `bytes`\n\nThe rest of the field list covers client IP, geo, host, user agent and OS.\n\n**Dashboard \"EFF-f8fb551c Web logs overview\"** (id `eff-f8fb551c-web-logs-overview-28bba30d80db`) has four panels:\n- Total requests\n- Requests over time\n- Responses by status code\n- Bytes by file extension\n\n**Rule \"EFF-f8fb551c 5xx spike\"** (id `89d9f893-a0ad-41b2-9482-89575a651a10`) is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It counts documents in the last 5 minutes where `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]`, and fires when that count is above 10. It has no actions attached, so a firing will show up in Kibana but won't send a notification anywhere.\n\nThe sample logs are historical, so the last 5 minutes may contain no matching documents. The rule may not fire unless fresh data arrives."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1182,"cache_read_input_tokens":19741,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1182},"output_tokens":459,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a4eb1cb7-866c-5bbf-8d81-a8d5acd83be3","uuid":"1013626f-fc43-4199-b2d3-11f12f790b7f","timestamp":"2026-10-12T00:53:23.024Z","request_id":"req_011CfwRr4GCYS63QqgL5YGWj"}
